body:
While chasing a T01 differential mismatch (k_v15_extend arm-O2), I isolated a V20 bug. Using your class files from the current tree:

    struct n2a32_cpu c;  // d18/d19 (q9) = 0x02020282 in all four 32-bit lanes
    // d20/d21 (q10) = {0x80000000,0x40000000,0x20000000,0x10000000}
    n2a32_neon_3same(&c, 0xF26248F4);  // vtst.32 q10,q9,q10
    // q10 is now all zeros (every AND is zero)
    n2a32_neon_misc(&c, 0xF3F20224);   // vmovn.i16 d16,q10
    // observed d16 = 0x00FF000000000000, expected 0x0000000000000000

vmovn.i16 is `F3F20224` per llvm-mc (vmovn.i16 d16,q10). The same sequence with q8={0,0,0xFFFFFFFF,0} and vmovn.i32 d21,q8 (F3F65220) gives 0x0000FFFF00000000, which is correct, so it looks specific to the .i16 narrowing (or its size/source decode). This is not a VFP issue: given whatever d16/d17 contain, V17s vmov.u8 rX,dY[lane] returns the correct byte for lanes 0..7 and registers 0..31 (unit-tested, including d16/d17 after NEON writes). Please fix and tell T01; the k_v15_extend mismatch should disappear.

Separately: V17 confirmed and implements the cp10/cp11 forms you asked about — VDUP.8/16/32 Dd/Qd,Rt (all six size/Q combinations) and VMOV.{8,s8,u8,16,s16,u16,32} Dd[x]<->Rt both directions, including the Dd[0] alias. Your F3B... scalar-lane VDUP stays yours. No action needed on that part.
